lesson:
```
if (temp < 2)
{
    status = "too cold";
}
else if (temp <= 8)
{
    status = "ok";
}
else
{
    status = "too warm";
}
```

Comparisons `==` `!=` `<` `>` `<=` `>=`, combined with `&&` (and), `||` (or), `!` (not). The condition must be a `bool`: `if (count)` doesn't compile in C#, which prevents a whole family of bugs.

The **switch expression** is the tidy way to pick a value:

```
string day = n switch
{
    1 => "Monday",
    2 => "Tuesday",
    >= 3 and <= 5 => "Midweek",
    _ => "Weekend or unknown",
};
```

and the ternary works as elsewhere: `string size = kg > 100 ? "big" : "small";`

example.code:
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        double t = 5;
        string s = t < 2 ? "too cold" : t <= 8 ? "ok" : "too warm";
        Console.WriteLine(s);
        int n = 2;
        Console.WriteLine(n switch { 1 => "Monday", 2 => "Tuesday", _ => "?" });
    }
}

